--TEST--
`Core\Task::afterResponse` runs its callable after the request's own execution, in registration order — `rule:concurrency/after-response-outlives-the-connection` and `rule:concurrency/deferred-is-bounded-by-two-directives`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// § 6: "The callable runs after the response is fully written." A CLI run has no
// response for anything to be *after*, so the request's own frame returning is
// when that is, and `nvs_runtime::deferred` is the one home for why all three
// hosts read the section the same way. The whole assertion here is *order*: a
// member that ran the callable at the registration would print `receipt sent`
// first, and one that only stored it would never print it at all.
Core\Task::afterResponse(fn (): void => {
    echo "receipt sent\n";
});
echo "response written\n";

// § 6 gives two calls no ordering but the one they were made in, and a queue
// that ran them backwards — or concurrently, which is the same thing to a case
// that only looks at stdout — would print these two the other way round.
Core\Task::afterResponse(fn (): void => {
    echo "then the webhook\n";
});

// § 7's `deadline` is the only option a deferred call takes: `limit` shapes
// concurrency and one callable has none. It is also the bound `[limits]
// wall_time` stops being, the client no longer being on the other end of it.
Core\Task::afterResponse(fn (): void => {
    echo "then the reindex, under a deadline\n";
}, {deadline: 30s});

// Registering neither consumes the callable nor freezes it: it stays a
// `callable` the program holds, so this one runs twice — once where it is
// called and once where it was deferred to.
var $twice = fn (): void => { echo "twice\n"; };
Core\Task::afterResponse($twice);
$twice();
echo "done\n";
--EXPECT--
response written
twice
done
receipt sent
then the webhook
then the reindex, under a deadline
twice
